Z||000000||Eddie.H||01-18-2000||05:55 PM||||I	DID my valve cover gasket Sunday on my 2.5 and you have to remove the EGR valve when doing this ok no prob. I put the valve gasket sealant on the valve cover then the gasket then I was letting it get a little sticky I then moved  on to the EGR valve I was about to put sealant on the new EGR valve gasket when I stoped to think to my self wait this gasket (the new one) my not be the right one because I replaced it (the EGR valve that is) with one from NAPA. They come with little washers that go in the middle of  THE EGR valve and the gasket .And the gasket for the NApA egr valve keeps the washer from dropping into the intake manifold the gasket that comes with the valve cover gasket is for a stock EGR valve and that gasket would alow the washer to fall right in . JUST A NOTE  Eddie||||reg||6
Z||000001||theogre||01-18-2000||10:25 PM||||I don't know what instructions you got.........<P>The washer is supposed to be staked, not heald by the gasket.... In other words once you select the proper metering washer and then you drop it into the recess in the EGR face. You then take a center punch, place it on the EGR face about 1mm from the washer and whack it. you do this in at least 3 locations around the washer to pinch the face of the EGR into the washer. <P>If you rely on only the gasket odds are you could have the washer inhaled by the motor.<P>Also, you don't use sealant on the EGR gasket. If you do make sure it's "sensor safe" or you could be replacing the O2 sensor soon.||||reg||1
